{
  "title": "The Event Loop",
  "summary": "One thread, a queue of jobs, and why setTimeout(fn, 0) runs later.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "One thread, many waits",
      "body": [
        "C# can run code on many threads at once. JavaScript runs your code on **one thread**. It stays responsive by never blocking: slow work (timers, network, files) is handed to the runtime, and your **callback** is queued to run when the result is ready.",
        "",
        "```text",
        " ┌──────────────┐      hands off timers, I/O, fetch       ┌─────────────────┐",
        " │  Call stack  │ ──────────────────────────────────────▶ │ Runtime (Node / │",
        " │ (your code)  │                                         │ browser)        │",
        " └──────┬───────┘                                         └────────┬────────┘",
        "        │ empty?                                                   │ done: queue",
        "        ▼                                                          ▼  the callback",
        " ┌──────────────────────┐   checked first   ┌──────────────────────────────────┐",
        " │ Microtask queue      │ ◀──────────────── │ Task queue                       │",
        " │ (promise callbacks)  │                   │ (setTimeout, I/O, click events)  │",
        " └──────────────────────┘                   └──────────────────────────────────┘",
        "```",
        "",
        "The loop: run the current script to the end, then run **all** microtasks, then take **one** task, then all microtasks again, and so on."
      ],
      "eventloop": {
        "code": [
          "console.log(\"start\");",
          "setTimeout(() => console.log(\"timer, 0 ms\"), 0);",
          "for (let i = 1; i <= 2; i++) console.log(\"loop\", i);",
          "console.log(\"end of script\");"
        ],
        "expect": [
          "start",
          "loop 1",
          "loop 2",
          "end of script",
          "timer, 0 ms"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "console.log(\"A\");",
        "setTimeout(() => console.log(\"B\"), 0);",
        "console.log(\"C\");"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "A",
        "C",
        "B"
      ],
      "explain": "`setTimeout(fn, 0)` does not run `fn` now; it queues a task. The task only runs after the current script finishes."
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Microtasks go first",
      "body": [
        "Promise callbacks (`.then`, and the code after an `await`) are **microtasks**. They run as soon as the current code finishes, **before** any timer:",
        "",
        "```ts",
        "setTimeout(() => console.log(\"timeout\"), 0);",
        "Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"promise\"));",
        "console.log(\"sync\");",
        "```",
        "",
        "```text",
        "sync",
        "promise",
        "timeout",
        "```",
        "",
        "Consequence: a long synchronous loop freezes everything, timers and clicks included. There is no other thread to rescue you. In a browser that is a frozen page."
      ],
      "eventloop": {
        "code": [
          "console.log(\"1 script\");",
          "setTimeout(() => console.log(\"5 timeout\"), 0);",
          "Promise.resolve()",
          "  .then(() => console.log(\"3 then\"))",
          "  .then(() => console.log(\"4 second then\"));",
          "queueMicrotask(() => console.log(\"3b queueMicrotask\"));",
          "console.log(\"2 script ends\");"
        ],
        "expect": [
          "1 script",
          "2 script ends",
          "3 then",
          "3b queueMicrotask",
          "4 second then",
          "5 timeout"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "setTimeout(() => console.log(1), 0);",
        "Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(2));",
        "queueMicrotask(() => console.log(3));",
        "console.log(4);"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "4",
        "2",
        "3",
        "1"
      ],
      "explain": "Synchronous code first (4), then microtasks in the order they were queued (2, 3), then the timer task (1)."
    },
    {
      "type": "sort",
      "prompt": "Where does each callback wait before it runs?",
      "buckets": [
        "microtask queue",
        "task queue"
      ],
      "items": [
        [
          "`.then(cb)` on a resolved promise",
          0
        ],
        [
          "`setTimeout(cb, 0)`",
          1
        ],
        [
          "code after `await`",
          0
        ],
        [
          "a button `click` handler",
          1
        ],
        [
          "`queueMicrotask(cb)`",
          0
        ],
        [
          "`setInterval(cb, 10)`",
          1
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "A page runs a synchronous loop that takes 3 seconds. What happens to a `setTimeout(fn, 100)` scheduled just before it?",
      "options": [
        "`fn` runs after the loop finishes, about 3 seconds late",
        "`fn` interrupts the loop after 100 ms",
        "`fn` runs on another thread at 100 ms",
        "`fn` is cancelled"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explain": "The delay is a **minimum**. Nothing can run until the call stack is empty."
    },
    {
      "type": "order",
      "prompt": "Arrange the lines so the program prints `1`, `2`, `3`, `4` in that order.",
      "lines": [
        "setTimeout(() => console.log(4), 0);",
        "Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(3));",
        "console.log(1);",
        "console.log(2);"
      ],
      "also": [
        [
          "setTimeout(() => console.log(4), 0);",
          "console.log(1);",
          "Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(3));",
          "console.log(2);"
        ],
        [
          "setTimeout(() => console.log(4), 0);",
          "console.log(1);",
          "console.log(2);",
          "Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(3));"
        ],
        [
          "Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(3));",
          "setTimeout(() => console.log(4), 0);",
          "console.log(1);",
          "console.log(2);"
        ],
        [
          "Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(3));",
          "console.log(1);",
          "setTimeout(() => console.log(4), 0);",
          "console.log(2);"
        ],
        [
          "Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(3));",
          "console.log(1);",
          "console.log(2);",
          "setTimeout(() => console.log(4), 0);"
        ],
        [
          "console.log(1);",
          "setTimeout(() => console.log(4), 0);",
          "Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(3));",
          "console.log(2);"
        ],
        [
          "console.log(1);",
          "setTimeout(() => console.log(4), 0);",
          "console.log(2);",
          "Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(3));"
        ],
        [
          "console.log(1);",
          "Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(3));",
          "setTimeout(() => console.log(4), 0);",
          "console.log(2);"
        ],
        [
          "console.log(1);",
          "Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(3));",
          "console.log(2);",
          "setTimeout(() => console.log(4), 0);"
        ],
        [
          "console.log(1);",
          "console.log(2);",
          "setTimeout(() => console.log(4), 0);",
          "Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(3));"
        ],
        [
          "console.log(1);",
          "console.log(2);",
          "Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(3));",
          "setTimeout(() => console.log(4), 0);"
        ]
      ],
      "explain": "Only the relative order of the two `console.log` lines matters: the microtask and the timer always run after them, microtask first. Any arrangement with `1` before `2` works."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Make it print in order",
      "prompt": [
        "Using exactly one `setTimeout(..., 0)`, one `Promise.resolve().then(...)` and one plain `console.log`, print:",
        "",
        "```text",
        "now",
        "soon",
        "later",
        "```"
      ],
      "starter": "// print now, soon, later",
      "solution": [
        "setTimeout(() => console.log(\"later\"), 0);",
        "Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"soon\"));",
        "console.log(\"now\");"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "expect": [
            "now",
            "soon",
            "later"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "require": [
        {
          "pattern": "setTimeout",
          "message": "Uses setTimeout"
        },
        {
          "pattern": "\\.then\\(",
          "message": "Uses a promise .then"
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "The timer prints `later`, the promise prints `soon`.",
        "The order you write them in does not matter here: the queues decide."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
